{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>You shall not hate your brother, etc.</b> That is, if something arose from your brother that causes you to hate him — do not hate him in your heart, but rather with your mouth speak to him, reproofs for what he has thus done, and this is what the verse says: <b>you shall reprove</b>, etc. And its saying <b>and you shall not bear sin because of him</b> means: do not decide in your mind that what arose from your brother he did in wickedness, and that he still stands in his sin laden upon his shoulders; rather, judge him favorably, and reprove him, and through the reproof one of two things will result: either he will give an excuse for what was done and there is no sin, or he will regret what was done and will resolve never to do such a thing again — and then he is as much a friend to you as a brother." "<b>Further,</b> it appears proper to explain, after raising an additional point regarding the formulation of the verse: it should have said ״לֹא תִשְׂנָא בִּלְבָבְךָ אֶת אָחִיךָ״ — completing the locus of hatred that it began speaking of, and only afterward mentioning whom one must not hate. Rather, it intends to convey the measure of hatred concerning which HaShem commands — lest a person say that only absolute hatred, seeking his harm and desiring his downfall, is called hatred, but mere distancing of the heart is not called hatred. Therefore Scripture was wise to juxtapose <b>לֹא תִשְׂנָא</b> with the word <b>אָחִיךָ</b>, to say that the threshold of hatred concerning which HaShem commands is that which is felt in relation to a brother, and this measure is such that whoever distances him from his heart even slightly — lo, he has descended from the level of a brother, and he thereby transgresses ״לֹא תִשְׂנָא״." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to raise the question of why in the prohibition ״לֹא תִשְׂנָא״ it specified ״אֶת אָחִיךָ״, whereas in the prohibition ״הוֹכֵחַ תּוֹכִיחַ״ it said ״אֶת עֲמִיתֶךָ״ — which appears to be speaking of two different subjects." "<b>However,</b> it may be explained according to the statement of the Sages, their memory for a blessing (Tikkunim 5; Tana deVei Eliyahu Rabbah, ch. 3): If you saw a Torah scholar who committed a transgression by day, do not harbor suspicions about him at night, for he has surely repented. We also find that they said, their memory for a blessing (Pesachim 113b), regarding the portion ״כִּי תִרְאֶה חֲמוֹר שׂוֹנַאֲךָ״, etc., that Scripture speaks of one who saw in his fellow something disgraceful, whom it is a mitzvah to hate. But here Scripture commanded that if one saw something disgraceful in a Torah scholar, he is not included in the category of those whom HaShem commanded to hate; rather, one must maintain that he has surely repented — and this is what it says: <b>לֹא תִשְׂנָא אֶת אָחִיךָ</b>, which refers to the Torah scholar, who is called by the name אָח, as it is written (Psalms 122:8): ״לְמַעַן אַחַי וְרֵעָי״. Or, along the lines of their saying that ״אֶת ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ תִּירָא״ includes Torah scholars — so too here <b>אֶת אָחִיךָ</b> means: that which is multiplied [i.e. included] by the את of אָחִיךָ, and the word אָחִיךָ refers back to the Creator, blessed be He, who is called a brother to Israel (Midrash Shocher Tov 23), as it were." "And its saying <b>הוֹכֵחַ תּוֹכִיחַ</b> speaks of a different subject — namely the multitudes of the people — and therefore it says <b>אֶת עֲמִיתֶךָ</b> and did not say ״הוֹכֵחַ תּוֹכִיחֶנּוּ״, reverting to the אָחִיךָ that it mentioned at the beginning. Rather, it says this to indicate that it is not speaking of what it began speaking of, namely the Torah scholar, but rather of the multitudes of the people, whom it is a mitzvah to hate — concerning them HaShem commands that one reprove them to their face: ״לָמָּה תַעֲשֶׂה כֹה״ — with pleasantness of speech, with affection and softness of tongue. And its saying <b>וְלֹא תִשָּׂא עָלָיו חֵטְא</b> means that one should not withhold reproof from him, for through this he will remain bearing his sin and will not return to HaShem. From this you learn that if one did not reprove him, he has nullified the positive commandment of הוֹכֵחַ and has violated the negative prohibition of ״וְלֹא תִשָּׂא״." "<b>Further</b>, it intends according to the saying of the Sages, may their memory be for blessing (Ketuvot 105a): 'This colleague [scholar] — his townspeople hate him because he rebukes them in matters of Heaven.' And from this the sage might say: 'Why do I need envy, why do I need hatred and rivalry with the seed of Israel? I shall refrain from rebuking and I shall be beloved and desirable to all of them!' Therefore HaShem was cunning, and before He commanded him to rebuke, He commanded the children of Israel not to hate him, and only afterward commanded him to rebuke — for after all, Israel are forewarned by HaShem not to hate him. And if you should say: 'And who will assure him that the people will not hate him and will observe the commandment of לֹא תִשְׂנָא?' — for this He said <b>and you shall not bear sin on his account</b>, meaning that he should not suspect them of this sin but rather judge them favorably. And it uses singular language to remove suspicion even from an individual, that he should hold every single Israelite as not hating the public rebuker, and with this he shall rebuke and go back and rebuke again until they accept his reproof." "<b>Further</b>, it intends according to the saying of the Sages, may their memory be for blessing (Shabbat 33a): 'For the sin of the generation, the great ones of the world are seized,' and as it says (Song of Songs 1:14): ״אֶשְׁכֹּל הַכֹּפֶר״ etc. And from this side, Scripture was concerned that a distancing of the heart might enter the hearts of those near to HaShem because of this deed of HaShem, for they might say that they are not secure in their lives and in their tranquility even though they make their deeds wondrously good. Therefore the word of HaShem came here and said to His righteous ones: ״לֹא תִשְׂנָא אֶת אָחִיךָ בִּלְבָבֶךָ״ — meaning, 'your brother' is the Holy One, blessed be He. And you should know that the Holy One, blessed be He, is not called 'brother' to all Israel equally, but only to those who are near and cleave to HaShem, like Rabbi Akiva and his colleagues. And He commanded them not to let their hearts distance from HaShem, for this distancing is a branch of hatred. And if you should say: 'What shall I answer my heart to trust in my life and my tranquility?' — for this He said <b>you shall surely rebuke your fellow</b>, and through this you shall not bear on his account — meaning, on his behalf — sin, for after all the reason for the seizure of the righteous is for the sin of the generation; if he rebukes him, he is saved either way: if they repent, then there are no sins for which to seize them, and if they do not repent, then you are exempt from seizure, as He revealed His secret to His servants the prophets, as it is written (Ezekiel 33:9): ״וְאַתָּה כִּי הִזְהַרְתָּ רָשָׁ״ and so forth — ״הוּא רָשָׁע בַּעֲוֹנוֹ יָמוּת וְאַתָּה אֶת נַפְשְׁךָ הִצַּלְתָּ״. And according to this approach, all the fine points we examined in the verse are resolved." "<b>Again</b>, there came to my hand a Tanna deVei Eliyahu (Rabbah, ch. 18), and this is its language: 'And does Scripture speak of brothers from the same father and mother? Scripture speaks not of them but against the Holy One, blessed be He, who has no brothers — not in this world, not in the world to come, and not in the days of the Messiah — except for the righteous of the world who do His will in His Torah every day, whom He called brothers and friends.' End of quotation. And you shall know that the rank of 'brother' is greater than the degree of 'children,' as the Sages, may their memory be for blessing, said (Song of Songs Rabbah, ch. 3): 'He did not depart from endearing her until He called her My daughter; He did not depart' and so forth 'until He called her My sister.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:59:45.528644390Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}